\section{What it is a covariance for}{
The sandwich is taken over the WHOLE outer parameter vector, so the
returned fixed-effect block already carries the cost of estimating
the covariance parameters. \code{clubSandwich::vcovCR()} on an \code{lmerMod}
instead conditions on the variance parameters - it sandwiches only
the mean-model bread - so the two agree exactly only when the
fixed-effect / covariance-parameter block of the observed
information vanishes. To reproduce that conditional form, build it
from \code{\link[=cluster_scores]{cluster_scores()}} and the fixed-effect block of \code{solve(vcov( object, full = TRUE))}.

\code{"CR2"} (Bell-McCaffrey) and \code{"CR3"} are refused rather than
approximated. Both are defined through the hat matrix of a linear
(or GLS) model; a Laplace-marginal likelihood with a nonlinear link
has no such representation, and nothing in the literature defines
the adjustment for one. Use \code{clubSandwich} on a matched linear model
if \code{"CR2"} is what the analysis needs.
}

\section{When it is refused}{
The marginal likelihood factors over clusters only when every random
effect is nested in (or equal to) the clustering factor, and when
every likelihood term is a product over rows. Fits that fail either
condition are refused with the reason: a random effect whose level
spans two clusters (including crossed effects, \code{mm()} pooled levels,
a global smooth, \code{gp()}, \code{car()} and the SPDE), a group-level
mixture whose groups span clusters, an \code{autocor()} residual, a
family whose structure does not declare \code{cluster_robust},
\code{rescor = TRUE}, \code{mi()}/\code{me()}, \code{REML = TRUE}, \code{profile = TRUE},
\code{quadrature = TRUE}, and any fit made with priors. \code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}}
is the fallback in every one of those cases.

Few clusters make the estimator badly biased whatever the
correction; the usual advice is at least 30-50, and \code{"CR1"} with a
\code{t(G - 1)} reference distribution below that. \code{\link[=confint]{confint()}} and
\code{\link[=hypothesis]{hypothesis()}} use the \code{t(G - 1)} reference automatically when they
are handed a matrix from this function.
}

